    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine you're building a new Lego castle. You start by gathering all the pieces, checking that you have everything you need, and making sure you're in the right place. That's kind of like what happens during mitosis, the process by which a cell divides into two identical daughter cells. It's divided into several stages, each with its own important job.\n\nFirst, the cell prepares for division by condensing its DNA into visible threads called chromosomes. This is called prophase. It's like gathering all the Lego pieces and organizing them into neat stacks.\n\nNext, the chromosomes line up in the middle of the cell, attached to the cell's spindle fibers. This is called metaphase. It's like arranging the Lego pieces in a row, making sure everything is in its right place.\n\nThen, the cell starts to divide the chromosomes into two groups, one for each daughter cell. This is called anaphase. It's like dividing the Lego pieces into two separate stacks.\n\nFinally, the cell splits into two daughter cells, each with a complete set of chromosomes. This is called telophase. It's like building the final castle, with all the Lego pieces in place.\n\nThroughout this process, the cell's organelles, like",
